1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,900
Right, Sandra. You wanted to see me to get some feedback on your group's proposal.

2
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:10,580
The one you're submitting for the Geography Society field trip competition.

3
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:15,630
I've had a look through your proposal and I think it's a really good choice.

4
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:19,879
In fact, I only have a few things to say about it,

5
00:00:19,879 --> 00:00:22,560
but even in an outline document like this

6
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:25,897
you really have to be careful to avoid typos

7
00:00:25,897 --> 00:00:30,500
and problems with layout in the proposal, and even in the contents page.

8
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:34,570
So read it through carefully before submitting it, okay?

9
00:00:34,570 --> 00:00:35,540
Will do.

10
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:39,740
And I've made a few notes on the proposal about things

11
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:41,730
which could have been better sequenced.

12
00:00:41,730 --> 00:00:42,690
Okay.

13
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:44,820
As for the writing itself

14
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:49,160
I've annotated the proposal as and where I thought it could be improved.

15
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:54,100
Generally speaking, I feel you've often used complex structures

16
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:56,097
and long sentences for the sake of it

17
00:00:56,097 --> 00:00:59,820
and as a consequence, although your paragraphing

18
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:01,401
and inclusion of sub- headings help,

19
00:01:01,401 --> 00:01:05,890
it's quite hard to follow your train of thought at times.

20
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:08,380
So cut them down a bit, can you?

21
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:09,280
Really?

22
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:13,850
Yes. And don't forget simple formatting like numbering.

23
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:16,140
Didn't I use page numbers?

24
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:18,745
I didn't mean that. Look,

25
00:01:18,745 --> 00:01:21,540
you've remembered to include headers and footers

26
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:27,080
which is good, but listing ideas clearly is important.

27
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:30,230
Number them or use bullet points, which is even clearer.

28
00:01:30,230 --> 00:01:33,880
Then you'll focus the reader on your main points.

29
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:39,350
I thought your suggestion to go to the Navajo Tribal Park was a very good idea.

30
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:41,920
I've always wanted to go there.

31
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:46,240
My father was a great fan of cowboy films and the Wild West

32
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:49,840
so I was subjected to seeing all the epics,

33
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:52,000
many of which were shot there.

34
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,880
As a consequence, it feels very familiar to me

35
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:58,867
and it's awesome both geographically and visually,

36
00:01:58,867 --> 00:02:02,300
so it's somewhere I've always wanted to visit.

37
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:08,210
The subsequent research I did and the online photographs made me even keener.

38
00:02:08,210 --> 00:02:14,400
Interesting. Right, let's look at the content of your proposal now.

39
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:16,890
Did you find it comprehensive enough?

40
00:02:16,890 --> 00:02:22,968
Well, yes and no. You've listed several different topics on your contents page,

41
00:02:22,968 --> 00:02:26,240
but I'm not sure they're all relevant.

42
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:31,132
No? Well, I thought that from the perspective of a field trip,

43
00:02:31,132 --> 00:02:36,840
one thing I needed to focus on was the sandstone plateaux and cliffs themselves.

44
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:41,360
The way they tower up from the flat landscape is just amazing.

45
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:46,597
The fact that the surrounding softer rocks were eroded by wind and rain,

46
00:02:46,597 --> 00:02:49,820
leaving these huge outcrops high above the plain.

47
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:53,720
It's hardly surprising that tourists flock to see the area.

48
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:57,890
Well, yes, I'd agree with including those points...

49
00:02:57,890 --> 00:03:01,564
And then the fact that it's been home to native American Navajos

50
00:03:01,564 --> 00:03:04,320
and all the social history that goes with that.

51
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:09,850
The hardships they endured trying to save their territory from the invading settlers.

52
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:14,370
Their culture is so rich - all those wonderful stories.

53
00:03:14,370 --> 00:03:20,022
Well, I agree it's interesting, but it's not immediately relevant to your proposal,

54
00:03:20,022 --> 00:03:25,240
Sandra, so at this stage, I suggest you focus on other considerations.

55
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:29,961
I think an indication of what the students on the trip could actually do

56
00:03:30,001 --> 00:03:32,994
when they get there should be far more central

57
00:03:32,994 --> 00:03:37,040
so that certainly needs to be included and to be expanded upon.

58
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:41,140
And I'd like to see something about the local wildlife,

59
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:45,650
and vegetation too, not that I imagine there's much to see.

60
00:03:45,650 --> 00:03:48,950
Presumably the tourist invasion hasn't helped.

61
00:03:48,950 --> 00:03:53,640
Okay, I'll do some work on those two areas as well.

62
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:59,350
But you're right, there's not much apart from some very shallow-rooted species.

63
00:03:59,350 --> 00:04:04,119
Although it's cold and snowy there in the winter, the earth is baked

64
00:04:04,119 --> 00:04:07,960
so hard in the summer sun that rainwater can't penetrate

65
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:11,090
So it's a case of flood or drought, really.

66
00:04:11,090 --> 00:04:16,960
So, I understand. Now, before we look at everything in more detail

67
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:19,770
I've got a few factual questions for you.

68
00:04:19,770 --> 00:04:24,768
It would be a good idea to include the answers in your finished proposal,

69
00:04:24,768 --> 00:04:26,910
because they're missing from your draft.

70
00:04:26,910 --> 00:04:27,710
Fine.

71
00:04:27,710 --> 00:04:30,852
So, you mentioned the monoliths and the spires,

72
00:04:30,852 --> 00:04:36,350
which was good, but what area does the tribal park cover? Do you know?

73
00:04:36,350 --> 00:04:46,100
12,000 hectares, and the plain is at about 5,850 metres above sea level.

74
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:51,570
Larger than I expected. Okay. Where's the nearest accommodation?

75
00:04:51,570 --> 00:04:54,500
That's a practical detail that you haven't included.

76
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:56,720
Have you done any research on that?

77
00:04:56,720 --> 00:05:00,400
Yes. There's nowhere to stay in the park itself

78
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:05,950
but there's an old trading post called Goulding quite near.

79
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:08,260
All kinds of tours start from Goulding, too.

80
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:09,820
What kind of tours?

81
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:14,760
Well, the most popular are in four-wheel drive jeeps

82
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:17,000
but I wouldn't recommend hiring those.

83
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,500
I think the best way to appreciate the area would be to hire horses instead

84
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:25,580
and trek around on those.

85
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:32,010
Biking is not allowed and it's impossible to drive around the area in private vehicles.

86
00:05:32,010 --> 00:05:34,280
The tracks are too rough.

87
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:38,450
Okay, lastly, what else is worth visiting there?

88
00:05:38,450 --> 00:05:44,200
There are several caves, but I haven't looked into any details.

89
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:46,160
I'll find out about them.

90
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:50,770
Okay, good. Now what I'd like to know is...
